Custom field serializers now accept their package-specific constructor options,
validate all configured defaults and choice payloads, and expose the base model's
validation metadata. Fields are selected using the model's configured type,
including independently for every item passed with ``many=True``.

Submitted values for fields belonging to another type or fields marked as not
editable now produce field-specific validation errors. API clients should use the
exposed ``editable``, validation, and type metadata to decide which custom fields
to render and submit.

Type selectors must be declared writable serializer fields that persist the
model's configured type relation. Heterogeneous serializers retain their typed
fields when representing validated ``many=True`` input.

Type selectors now use their configured DRF representation, validate compatible
relation sources before saving, and honor selector defaults. Choice defaults are
validated only when applied to input, avoiding per-item database lookups during
list serialization.

Mapped lists now retain per-item type selection without applying mapping hooks
twice. Saved serializers immediately represent newly written custom values, and
full and partial updates preserve all omitted custom values. Custom-field defaults
are create-only. Mapping serializers which exclude custom fields ignore mapped
custom values while continuing to process normal model fields.

Multiple-choice validation now materializes its validated choices and compares
JSON lookup values with type-sensitive identities, so values such as ``true`` and
``1`` remain distinct.

Custom-field identifiers that collide with model fields, relation attnames,
runtime model attributes, or serializer fields are now rejected with
``ImproperlyConfigured`` before annotations or serializer fields are built. This
check includes filtered, excluded, and type-specific definitions.
